<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Piltene</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Pilten</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Piltene, in German Pilten, is a small town on the Venta river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Kurzeme</a>, western Latvia. Its castle was a residence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of Courland. After the bishopric was sold to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> in 1559, Magnus of Holstein ruled from it and died there in 1583, and the surrounding District of Pilten remained a separate territory under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Polish crown</a> until 1795.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/piltene.jpg" alt="Piltene" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: original download by Kikos, transferred to the Commons by Furfur · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Piltenes_pilsdrupas_un_bazn%C4%ABca.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Piltene, in German Pilten and in Polish Piltyń, is a small town in Ventspils Municipality in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a> region of western Latvia. It had a population of 909 in 2020. The ruins of Piltene Castle stand in the town, which gave its name to a territory that was, over several centuries, a bishopric, a district under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Polish crown</a> and a possession of the dukes of Courland.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>According to the recorded chronology, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valdemar-ii-of-denmark">Valdemar II of Denmark</a> built a castle on the site in 1220. The Bishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a> was established in 1234, after being declared in 1232, in three separate enclaves made up from lands divided among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of Courland and Riga and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a>. The bishops also ruled the island of Runö, now Ruhnu in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>, from at least 1341. In 1290 the cathedral chapter was incorporated into the lands of the Teutonic Order, and the bishopric came under the Order. In 1520 it became a sovereign prince-bishopric of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, formally from January 1521, though the bishops did not use the style of prince.</p><p>The bishopric was sold to the King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, who gave it as an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a> to his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnus-duke-of-holstein">Magnus, Duke of Holstein</a>. In 1578 Bishop Magnus accepted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> of Poland-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, which the Polish Diet did not ratify and Denmark did not recognise. Denmark sold the territory to Poland-Lithuania on 20 April 1585, and in June of that year informal subdivisions were set up: Pilten, Dondangen and Amboten. In 1611 the incorporation of Pilten into Poland-Lithuania as a district was declared but not carried out, and in 1617 the bishopric entered a personal union with Poland-Lithuania that was in practice a subordinate one.</p><p>The Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a> bought the rights of pledge in 1656 and received Pilten as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> of Poland-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> on 3 May 1660, with a formal personal union with Courland in 1661. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> occupied it from 1701 to 1709, with a Russian occupation in 1705 and 1706. The union with the Commonwealth was restored in 1717. On 28 March 1795 the Pilten Landtag approved annexation to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and declared the suzerainty of Poland-Lithuania void, and Russia annexed it on 26 April 1795, guaranteeing its autonomy. Autonomy was abolished in 1796, restored in 1797, and the district was fully incorporated into Courland on 2 March 1818. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> occupied it briefly in 1812.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Piltene” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The bishop's lands centred on <b>Pilten</b> and Hasenpoth, with the cathedral at Hasenpoth (Aizpute).</blockquote><a href="/state/bishopric-of-courland">Bishopric of Courland</a></li><li><blockquote>The district, scattered around <b>Pilten</b> (Piltene) and Hasenpoth in western Latvia, was not merged into the Duchy of Courland and Semigallia but held directly by the Polish crown.</blockquote><a href="/state/district-of-pilten">District of Pilten</a></li><li><blockquote>Magnus fled to Polish protection in 1578, renounced his royal title and died at <b>Pilten</b> in 1583.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-livonia">Kingdom of Livonia</a></li></ul></section>
